    <statement><p>Which of the following two equations
defines a horizontal line?</p>
<p>Write "T" if the equation defines a horizontal line, "F" otherwise.</p>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p><m>r=6\csc\theta</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>r=6\sec\theta</m></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <solution><m>r=6 \csc \theta</m> is the polar equation of a horizontal line,
as it can be written as <m>r=6/\sin  \theta</m>, so
<m>r \sin  \theta = 6</m>, which becomes <m>y=6</m>.



On the other hand,
the equation
<m>r=6 \sec \theta</m> is the polar equation of a vertical line,
as it can be written as <m>r=6/\cos  \theta</m>, so
<m>r \cos  \theta = 6</m>, which becomes <m>x=6</m>.





</solution>
